@page "/docs/cli/crud"

@* izzy add crud <Model> (catalog/crud in src/IzzyBlazor.Cli): the grid page it writes, how it reads the model,
   the store, the options, and Pro's --mode pages (artifacts/izzyblazor-crud-design.md). *@

<ArticlePage Section="cli" Slug="crud">
    <Lead>
        <Code>izzy add crud</Code> writes a page to list, add, edit and delete a model's records, for internal tools and
        admin screens. Name a class from your app; izzy reads it from the source, so the app doesn't have to build first.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Add the page</Heading>
        <Code Block Language="bash">izzy add crud Customer</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            That writes a page at <Code>/customers</Code> whose grid searches, sorts and pages the customers, and adds and
            edits them in a dialog checked against the model's attributes; delete asks first. Columns lets each person
            choose the columns, kept in a cookie, and Export downloads what's shown as an Excel file, from an endpoint in
            <Code>Data/CustomerExport.cs</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            It works on a static page, with no circuit, and on an interactive one. The page needs signing in when the app
            has accounts.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Where the records are kept</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The page reads and writes the records through <Code>ICustomerStore</Code>: through your DbContext when one has
            a <Code>DbSet&lt;Customer&gt;</Code>, otherwise in memory until the app stops. <Code>--store</Code> chooses.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Labels and fields</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Labels come from <Code>[Display(Name)]</Code>, or the property's name in words; column titles from
            <Code>ShortName</Code>. A key the database makes, read-only properties and computed ones stay out of the form.
            Related records, such as a customer's orders, aren't written; izzy names each property it leaves out.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            A key people choose, such as an invoice number, is asked for on New and fixed after, on the Pro pages.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Options</Heading>
        <Table Items="CrudOptions" Caption="izzy add crud options" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Option" Width="Length.Rem(14)"><code>@context!.Name</code></TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="What it does">@context!.Text</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>

        <Heading Level="2">Full screens with Pro</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            With IzzyBlazor Pro, <Code>--mode pages</Code> writes the full screens instead: a list with filters and empty
            states, a page for each customer, and a form page with the errors listed at the top. It suits records with
            many fields.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">After the model changes</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Run the command again with <Code>--diff</Code> to see what changes, and <Code>--overwrite</Code> to take them.
            See <Anchor Href="docs/cli/changes">Previewing changes</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private sealed record Row(string Name, string Text);

    private static readonly Row[] CrudOptions =
    [
        new("--mode pages", "Pro: a list with filters, a page for each record and a form page, for records with many fields. Without it, one grid page."),
        new("--name <Plural>", "The folder, the pages' names and the title. Defaults to the model's plural: Customers."),
        new("--singular <words>", "What one record is called on the pages, when it isn't the class's name: --singular \"team member\" for Member. --plural sets the other, when adding an s isn't it."),
        new("--route <path>", "The address the pages start at. Defaults to the name in kebab case: customers."),
        new("--store ef|memory", "Where the records are kept. ef needs a DbSet of the model; --context picks the DbContext when two have one."),
        new("--authorize", "Only signed-in people can open the pages. On when the app has accounts."),
    ];
}
